all right let's jump uh jump right into  this topic it's a big topic uh it's a  complicated one over the last several  weeks i've gotten a ton of emails from  you guys uh calls  with people asking or suggesting uh  maybe if you are unvaccinated and you  get very sick with covet and you go to  the hospital you should be refused  treatment um and of course that's not  going to happen in any real way there  are uh primary care doctors that are  saying our practice will only treat  we'll only have on our roster vaccinated  patients but if you go to an emergency  room you are not going to be denied  treatment because you are unvaccinated  for better or worth worse ethical  unethical it that's just not going to  happen but there are now  new questions it's a much more narrow  discussion and the discussion is whether  it would be fair to treat vaccinated  coveted patients first and this is more  of a triage conversation so really it's  a question of whether vaccination status  should or could be considered in terms  of triage and there's some pretty good  arguments to suggest that it should  uh which we are going to get into uh  interesting wired article which asks  would it be fair to treat vaccinated  coveted patients first  last week texas healthcare policymakers  discussed taking vaccination status into  account for covetrt triage it's a larger  conversation ethicists are bracing for  um  it's no accident that people describe  something that's calculated cold and  unemotional as clinical taking care of  people's health isn't it health isn't  emotion free by any means  but the clinic is where physicians and  nurses have to make life and death  decisions under time pressure including  which patients go first and what kind of  care they get when resources like say  ventilators or intensive care beds  oxygen staff and time or scarce  that decision making is called triage  and last week the dallas morning news  reported  that some texas hospitals were  considering a massive change to how they  do it the question in play  would it make sense to take into account  the vaccination status of their covet  patients with icus filling up with  severely ill coveted patients and a  shortage of beds and nurses should  clinicians all other things being equal  that's an important part of it  care for vaccinated people before or  differently than unvaccinated ones and  the article goes on to say it hasn't  happened and probably won't the idea  came from a memo  this is this is a very complicated  question  fundamentally this is not about ignoring  the hippocratic oath and saying don't  treat unvaccinated patients  uh they haven't taken care of themselves  assuming there's no real medical reason  why they are unvaccinated  we're going to ignore these people that  that's not what this is about  this is about  including vaccination status as part of  triage  where someone may end up getting less  care or might end up getting no care  depending on how full an emergency room  is in a moment i'll tell you about three  emergency rooms that are so full they've  closed  um and whether  the vaccination status affects chance of  recovery uh and you could on this basis  say all right you've got  a 65 year old  vaccinated patient  and a 60 year old unvaccinated patient  and if you don't consider vaccination  status you might just go by how severe  are their symptoms  their age etc if you consider  vaccination status you would have this  statistical framework in which you know  that hospitalized vaccinated patients  are more likely to survive than  unvaccinated patients that would be a  way to empirically  not in some kind of moralistic  punishment way but empirically consider  vaccination status in triage is a  vaccinated 50 year old more likely to  recover  than an unvaccinated  40 year old and there's even another  aspect of this which is if you have a  patient in the er or in the icu or  potentially going into the icu  and they're unvaccinated and they're  unvaccinated for no other reason other  than they fell for conspiracy theories  does it stand to reason that they may  generally be less likely to heed the  advice that they are given in that  hospital if granted an intensive care  unit would it be fair to consider that  as well and of course there's there's  the important inclusion of the idea all  else being equal and really the the i  think the gist here  is not to make  unclear triage calls about a vaccinated  55 year old and an unvaccinated 40 year  old but really more about situations  where it's  almost identical situations you might  have two people  equally sick  same age  one vaccinated one unvaccinated and you  have one spot in the icu would it be  fair to say we're going to take the  vaccinated patient because statistically  they're more likely to recover and in  general they are probably more likely to  heed our advice this is not  unheard of  elsewhere  uh on the basis of a a different sort of  sort of health considerations alcoholics  historically  have been considered unsuitable for  liver transplants because of the  presumed high risk of relapsing to  excessive drinking which could ruin the  transplanted liver that's that's a  lifestyle choice that is factored into  organ donation although there are people  who rightly say well it's not really a  lifestyle choice alcoholism is a disease  and then it becomes more complex but the  point is that it is it's similar it's  analogous to some degree triage is about  chances of survival but it's also about  available resources it's also about  patient willingness to comply with  medical advice and people who are  vaccinated are not only more likely to  survive they've shown a motivation  to follow the widely accepted medical  advice and ultimately this is about  available resources and we've talked  before and i'm going to give you an  example in the next story of this  when you have a full icu  with disproportionately unvaccinated  people not because of a medical  condition that says don't get vaccinated  but because they've chosen not to get  vaccinated if you get into a car  accident if you have a heart attack if  you have a work injury  and you're vaccinated and you go in for  something serious not related to covid  you may not be able to get the treatment  you need because of people who have  simply chosen not to get vaccinated and  so there is an element of this which is  that people who are not looking out for  society are consuming resources and  hurting other people people who maybe  would survive after that car crash if  they could get right into an icu but the  icu is full because of unvaccinated  people so i think it's completely  reasonable to take it into account in  triage i'm not saying you don't provide  treatment  but the question about triage  considerations seems completely logical  to me  one of our sponsors is privacy.com  they're giving you five dollars when you  sign up for their completely free  service at privacy.com slash pacman i've  been using privacy for a little over a  year now you've heard me talk about it  before it's a lifesaver and here's how  it works takes just a couple of minutes  to set up anytime you buy something  online or on the phone  instead of actually using your real  credit card number the privacy app and  the browser plug-in  let you give each company a randomized  virtual credit card number that you  create out of thin air it'll even  autofill the card number with one click  and the payment is taken out of your  checking account without the merchant  ever knowing your real information so  this allows you to keep your banking  information secure but also to take  control of your finances you can create  up to 12 of these virtual credit cards a  month you can set spending limits you  can freeze them you can delete them  anytime you want so when you do this it  means you're not going to be charged  when you don't want to be because you  can destroy the virtual card number 